I'll search the codebase for the verdict parser implementation.**File:** `/Users/nick/Desktop/vigil/src/plan/verdict.ts`

**Exposed parsing function:** `parseVerdictLines(text: string): VerdictLine[]`

### How it works

It scans input line-by-line (`text.split(/\r?\n/)`) and matches against three regexes, in priority order:

| Order | Regex | Produces |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | `PLAN_RE` | `{ type: "plan", planId, verdict: "pass" \| "fail" }` |
| 2 | `GATE_RE` | `{ type: "gate", gateId, verdict, reason? }` |
| 3 | `TASK_RE` | `{ type: "task", taskId }` |

Notable details:
- All patterns tolerate surrounding Markdown noise via `[`*_]*` — so `**VG-001: PASS**` parses fine.
- Gate IDs are normalized with `.toUpperCase()`; verdicts normalized with `.toLowerCase()`.
- The gate `reason` is the trailing capture group, stripped of leading separators (`-`, en/em dashes, colons, whitespace), and set to `undefined` if empty.
- Matching is `continue`-based, so each line yields **at most one** verdict — plan lines win over gate lines, which win over task lines.

### Companion export

The same file exports `LineBuffer`, an incremental splitter for streamed agent output. `push(chunk)` returns only complete lines and retains the trailing partial line in `this.buf`; `flush()` drains the remainder. This lets the runner feed streaming SDK text into `parseVerdictLines` without splitting a verdict mid-line.

### Related call sites

The parser is consumed in `src/engine/runner.ts` and `src/engine/session.ts`, with the emitted contract text (the instructions telling the agent to print these lines) generated by `runnerContract()` in `/Users/nick/Desktop/vigil/src/plan/parser.ts:107`. Tests live in `tests/parser.test.ts`, `tests/verdict-rails.test.ts`, and `tests/session-verdict.test.ts`.

One thing worth flagging: since the regexes are anchored at line start but permissive about decoration, an agent quoting a verdict inside a code block or narrating "I will print VG-001: PASS" could produce a false positive. That overlaps with your backlog item on rail evaluation hardening.

